    script: 'When we tested the first version of our garden audio tour, one visitor said that it felt as though someone was standing beside her, pointing at every single plant. At first, I took that as a complaint about the amount of information. We cut a few facts, but the next group still had the same reaction. Then I watched people using the tour. They were trying to look at a plant while the recording was already telling them about the next one. The information itself wasn’t especially difficult; the recording simply never gave them space to notice anything for themselves. So we changed the instructions. Instead of saying, “On your left you’ll see a red flower,” we sometimes ask people to find something that catches their attention. We then leave a short silence before continuing. Some colleagues were nervous about that. They thought listeners might assume the recording had stopped working. We tested the pauses, and that didn’t happen. What did happen was that people began mentioning things afterwards that weren’t in our script at all. We’ve kept the factual sections, because people do want to learn about the garden. But the tour now makes fewer decisions about what they should notice at every moment.',
