    title: 'An actor on recording a familiar novel', speakers: ['Narrator'],
    script: 'People sometimes ask whether recording a book I loved as a teenager was easier because I already knew it so well. In one sense, of course it was. I knew where the story was going, and I had a strong feeling for its rhythm. The difficulty was that I also remembered how I wanted it to sound. In my memory, the central character was wonderfully decisive. She seemed to say the things I hadn’t yet learned how to say. Reading the book aloud twenty years later, I kept finding little hesitations in her speech. She changes the subject. She makes a joke just when somebody expects an answer. I’d somehow edited those moments out.\n\nMy first instinct was to protect the version of her I remembered. I gave the hesitant lines the same confidence as everything else. It sounded quite convincing in the studio, which was part of the problem. The performance was smoothing away a distinction the writing had taken care to make. Our producer stopped me after one scene and asked whether the character believed what she’d just said, or needed the other person to believe it. I couldn’t answer immediately.\n\nThat became a useful question throughout the recording. It didn’t mean turning every confident line into a secret confession of doubt. That would have been another way of imposing a single explanation. We tried instead to leave the shifts audible: sometimes she knows exactly what she means; sometimes the certainty is something she borrows for the duration of a conversation. I still recognise the character I admired. I just think admiration had made me listen selectively. Familiarity helped me enter the book, but it wasn’t a reliable guide to every turn within it.',
